-- Original foundation curriculum. These are learning exercises, not official or recalled recruitment questions.
begin;

Worked example: A fee increases from 800 to 920. The increase is 120, so the percentage increase is 120/800 × 100 = 15%. Dividing by 920 answers a different question: the increase as a share of the new fee.

Successive changes multiply. A 20% rise followed by a 20% fall changes 100 into 120 and then 96: a net 4% fall. For rates a and b written as decimals, the final multiplier is (1+a)(1+b).

Reverse percentages: after a 25% discount, an item costs 600. The sale price is 75% of the original, so original price = 600/0.75 = 800. Do not add 25% to 600.

Method: name the base, write the multiplier, calculate, then check whether the direction and size are sensible. Practise below without a calculator first.') on conflict(lesson_id) do nothing;

Worked example: A takes 12 days and B takes 18 days. Their combined rate is 1/12 + 1/18 = 5/36 jobs per day. Together they need 36/5 = 7.2 days. The result should be faster than either worker alone.

For partial work, multiply rate by time. If A works alone for 3 days, A completes 3/12 = 1/4, leaving 3/4. At the combined rate 5/36, finishing that remaining work takes (3/4)/(5/36) = 5.4 more days.

Pipes use the same model: filling is positive and draining is negative. A pipe fills a tank in 6 hours and a drain empties it in 9 hours. Net rate is 1/6 − 1/9 = 1/18 tank per hour, so filling takes 18 hours.

State assumptions: equal-length working days, constant rates and no interference. Keep fractions until the last step; early rounding can change the answer.') on conflict(lesson_id) do nothing;

Start with first differences. In 3, 7, 13, 21, the differences are 4, 6, 8. If the differences increase by 2, the next difference is 10 and the next term is 31.

Next inspect ratios: 2, 6, 18, 54 multiplies by 3 each time. Also test alternating subsequences, such as the odd-position terms separately from the even-position terms. A sequence need not use the same operation at every position.

For square-based sequences, compare n² or n²+1 with the terms. For 2, 5, 10, 17, the terms are 1²+1, 2²+1, 3²+1, 4²+1, suggesting 26 next.

Write the proposed rule and verify every transition. Under timed conditions, skip a sequence when multiple plausible rules remain; return after answering more certain questions.') on conflict(lesson_id) do nothing;

For objects, == compares reference identity. String.equals compares character content. If a = new String("hi") and b = new String("hi"), a == b is false while a.equals(b) is true. String literals may be interned, so use equals when content equality is intended rather than relying on identity.

An instance method overridden by a subclass is selected using the runtime object type. If Animal a = new Dog(), calling an overridden a.speak() invokes Dog.speak(). Overloading chooses among different parameter lists at compile time; it is a different mechanism.

Strings are immutable: concatenation creates a result rather than changing an existing String object. Repeated building in a loop can use StringBuilder when mutation is appropriate.

Interview approach: identify whether the expression concerns a primitive, a reference or object state, then separate compile-time type checking from runtime dispatch. For null references, calling an instance method throws NullPointerException.') on conflict(lesson_id) do nothing;

Example: employees(id, name, department_id) and departments(id, name). To list every employee, including those without a department, use SELECT e.name, d.name FROM employees e LEFT JOIN departments d ON d.id = e.department_id.

Be careful with filters. Adding WHERE d.name = ''Sales'' after this LEFT JOIN discards rows where d.name is NULL. If you need to retain every employee while attaching only a Sales department match, put that condition in the ON clause instead.

One-to-many joins can multiply rows. Joining a customer to three orders yields three matching pairs. COUNT(*) counts joined rows; COUNT(column) counts non-NULL values of that column. Use the intended grouping and check for duplication before calculating totals.

To find employees with no department match, use the LEFT JOIN followed by WHERE d.id IS NULL. Compare NULL with IS NULL, never = NULL. SQL NULL represents missing or unknown information, not an empty string or zero.') on conflict(lesson_id) do nothing;

Before scanning, define empty-input behaviour. For a maximum, an empty array has no answer: return an optional result or report an error according to the function contract. Do not silently return zero.

A linear scan has O(n) time and O(1) extra space if it stores only a few variables. Sorting first costs more work and can mutate the input, so it is unnecessary for finding a single maximum.

Worked example: [-7, -2, -9]. Start with -7; compare -2 and replace the best; compare -9 and keep -2. The invariant is that best is the maximum of the prefix seen so far.

Test cases: empty input, one element, all equal, all negative, duplicates of the maximum and a maximum at either end. For sums, also consider whether the language''s integer type can overflow.') on conflict(lesson_id) do nothing;

All A are B means the A set is contained in B. It does not say that all B are A. No A are B means the two sets do not overlap. Some A are B asserts at least one object in their overlap.

Worked example: all roses are flowers; all flowers are plants. Every rose must be a plant because subset relationships are transitive. However, these premises do not say that every plant is a rose.

If all coders are readers and some readers are musicians, it does not follow that some coders are musicians. The musician readers could all be outside the coder set. Drawing that counterexample is enough to disprove the proposed conclusion.

In standard predicate logic, a universal statement does not by itself assert that its subject set contains any members. When an assessment uses a different existence convention, follow its stated instructions. For unambiguous practice, distinguish explicitly existential ''some'' claims from universal claims.') on conflict(lesson_id) do nothing;

For coordinated subjects joined by and, use a plural verb in ordinary cases: ''Asha and Ravi are ready.'' Expressions such as each and every ordinarily take singular agreement: ''Each student has a notebook.''

Keep parallel structure when listing activities. ''She enjoys reading, coding and solving puzzles'' uses three matching -ing forms. Mixing a noun, an infinitive and a clause unnecessarily makes the list harder to read.

Pronouns should refer clearly to a noun. A sentence like ''Ravi told Arun that he had passed'' can be ambiguous; use the person''s name when the intended reference is unclear.

To review a sentence: find its subject and main verb, temporarily remove intervening phrases, check agreement, then check tense and parallelism. Do not choose a correction solely because it sounds more formal. Preserve the original meaning.') on conflict(lesson_id) do nothing;

For an inclusive interval [low, high], continue while low <= high. Compute mid = low + floor((high-low)/2). If a[mid] equals the target, return mid. If a[mid] is smaller, set low = mid+1; otherwise set high = mid-1. Each update must make the interval smaller.

Worked example: find 11 in [2, 5, 8, 11, 14]. The initial middle is 8, so discard the left half including 8. The remaining interval contains 11 and 14; the next middle can identify 11.

The loop invariant is that if the target exists, it is inside the remaining interval. When low > high, the interval is empty and the target was absent. With duplicates, this basic algorithm may return any matching index. Finding the first match requires keeping a candidate and continuing left.

Time complexity is O(log n); an iterative version uses O(1) extra space. Test empty and one-element arrays, absent values, endpoints and duplicates. Be consistent about inclusive or half-open intervals instead of mixing their update rules.') on conflict(lesson_id) do nothing;

Choose one or two topics for the next study session. Read a worked lesson, close it, and explain the method in your own words. Then solve a few new questions without referring back. If you cannot explain why an option is correct, revisit the concept before increasing speed.

Review unanswered questions separately. They may indicate a pacing problem rather than a knowledge gap. Practise deciding when to skip, and reserve time at the end to return to marked questions.

Retake a comparable test after studying. Compare accuracy, unanswered questions and time usage together. A higher score achieved only by remembering the answer key is weaker evidence than solving fresh questions correctly. Use several sessions before drawing conclusions about a topic.

PlacementWays recommendations use graded answers to suggest areas to review. Treat them as a starting point for planning; a small sample cannot capture every part of your understanding.','published') on conflict(slug) do nothing;
commit;

-- Executable contracts and examples for the original coding exercises.
update public.questions set question_text=question_text || E'\n\nExecution contract: input is a count n (1 <= n <= 100000), then n space-separated integers. Print the maximum integer followed by a newline.' where slug='maximum-array-scan' and question_text not like '%Execution contract:%';
update public.questions set question_text=question_text || E'\n\nExecution contract: input is n and target on the first line, then n ascending integers on the second line. n may be zero. Print the zero-based first matching index or -1.' where slug='first-binary-search-match' and question_text not like '%Execution contract:%';
update public.questions set question_text=question_text || E'\n\nExecution contract: read one line (excluding its line terminator). Print true or false in lowercase. An empty line is a palindrome; compare case and spaces exactly.' where slug='palindrome-two-pointers' and question_text not like '%Execution contract:%';
select q.id,v.input,v.output,v.sample,v.position from public.questions q join (values
 ('maximum-array-scan',E'3\n-7 -2 -9\n',E'-2\n',true,1),('maximum-array-scan',E'1\n0\n',E'0\n',false,2),('maximum-array-scan',E'5\n4 9 9 -2 7\n',E'9\n',false,3),
 ('first-binary-search-match',E'5 2\n1 2 2 2 5\n',E'1\n',true,1),('first-binary-search-match',E'0 8\n\n',E'-1\n',false,2),('first-binary-search-match',E'4 3\n1 2 4 7\n',E'-1\n',false,3),
 ('palindrome-two-pointers',E'level\n',E'true\n',true,1),('palindrome-two-pointers',E'Level\n',E'false\n',false,2),('palindrome-two-pointers',E'\n',E'true\n',false,3)
) as v(slug,input,output,sample,position) on v.slug=q.slug on conflict(question_id,position) do nothing;
